  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 324 PM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nToday's upper level pattern features benign northwest flow on the \nperiphery of strong ridging anchored over the central CONUS. GOES \nvisible satellite reveals mostly clear skies across Upper Michigan \nand Lake Superior as of mid afternoon as surface high pressure \npersists over the region, with temperatures warming into low to mid \n80s in most locations away from the lakeshores. A few of the more \nexcitable CAMs have continued to depict a few showers and \nthunderstorms popping up on the lake breeze later this afternoon, \nwhich remains plausible given around 500 J/kg SBCAPE over the \ncentral and western UP per SPC mesoanalysis. Have thus held onto 20-\n30% chance PoPs in the forecast through the remaining daylight \nhours, although hourly HRRR updates continue to keep us dry for the \nrest of the day. Will continue to keep an eye on satellite and radar \ntrends to see if anything pops up through the afternoon, but either \nway do not expect any strong to severe storms given weak shear and \nrather limited instability.\n\nTemperatures trend upward on Thursday as the ridge axis acquires a \npositive tilt and shifts eastward to the Great Lakes, yielding 500 \nmb height rises and 850 mb temps warming to 18-20 C. This will \ncorrespond to highs in the mid to upper 80s over much of the area, \nwith a few spots pushing 90 near the WI border. This will once again \nyield some modest instability and a 20-30% chance for showers and \nthunderstorms as a pair of disturbances over northern Ontario and \nthe Northern Plains, respectively, approach late in the day. The \nsouthern stream disturbance will move across Lower Michigan on Friday \nand interact with a cold front extending from the low over northern \nOntario, bringing increased rain and thunderstorm chances to the UP \non Friday as high temperatures dip back into the 70s. Chances for a \nwetting rain of at least a quarter inch range from 50-70% across the \nwestern and central UP through Friday night per the NBM, with closer \nto a 30-50% chance in the east. A few showers and thunderstorms \ncould linger into early Saturday in the wake of these systems. Do \nnot expect any strong to severe storms for any of these periods as \nshear remains weak for the next few days.\n\nSunday into Monday favors drier weather a high pressure builds back \ninto the region, with temperatures looking to climb back into the \n80s for much of next week. Another approaching trough will bring \nincreasing precipitation chances again towards the middle of next \nweek, but details are sparse at this time.",
